// EXPECT: E0250 — `using` on a union (ADR-0050 §5)
//
// ADR-0045 §6 said "no `using` on a union" when `using` did not exist, which was a statement about
// absence rather than a decision. ADR-0050 §5 decides it on the merits and the answer is still no,
// for a reason: a union is untagged, so exactly one field holds a valid value and nothing records
// which — and a *promoted* `i` gives the reader no syntactic clue a union is involved, where an
// explicit `b.i` does.
//
// A tagged variant type would overturn this, because a tag makes "which field is valid"
// answerable. That is ADR-0045 §1's deferred decision, not this one.
Bits :: union {
    i: s64;
    f: float64;
}

read_it :: (using b: Bits) -> s64 {
    return i;
}
